Chlorophyll: empty pooled blocks are kept for a while, and a placement up to 3/4 of a block is pooled.

backboardd (CoreAnimation) takes several framebuffer-sized targets per animation (828x1792 RGBA16F = 12 MB) and drops them
after it. The runtime freed each emptied block past three and made new ones at the next animation: about three 16 MB
kernel allocations per home-screen page swipe, 60-80 ms each in our kernel, on the display thread (35 % of its CPU time),
and the frees on the completion thread. Now three empty blocks are always kept and, past three, the one empty the longest
is freed once it has been empty for 5 s; a 12 MB target takes a kept 16 MB block instead of an allocation of its own.

